<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Pernambuco</h1><p class="st-meta">1817 CE – 1817 CE · capital Recife</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived republic proclaimed in Recife in 1817, the first serious attempt to break part of Brazil away from the Portuguese crown.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pernambuco's planters and merchants resented the heavy taxes imposed to support the royal court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, and Masonic lodges spread liberal and republican ideas. On 6 March 1817 officers resisting arrest killed their commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a>, and a provisional republican government took power.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The provisional government, including the merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domingos-jose-martins">Domingos José Martins</a> and the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joao-ribeiro">João Ribeiro</a>, abolished some taxes, proclaimed freedom of conscience and the press, and drafted an organic law. It sent an envoy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-goncalves-da-cruz">Antônio Gonçalves da Cruz</a>, to seek recognition from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>. The revolt spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraiba">Paraíba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-norte">Rio Grande do Norte</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was left untouched to keep planter support.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Royal forces of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-brazil">Kingdom of Brazil</a> blockaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a> by sea and advanced overland from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>. The republic fell in May 1817, after about 75 days. Several leaders, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domingos-jose-martins">Martins</a>, were executed, and others, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frei-caneca">Frei Caneca</a>, imprisoned, later joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-equator">Confederation of the Equator</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-pernambuco.jpg" alt="Republic of Pernambuco" width="320" height="376"><figcaption>Image: Antônio Parreiras · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B%C3%AAn%C3%A7%C3%A3o_das_bandeiras_da_Revolu%C3%A7%C3%A3o_de_1817.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1817 CE – 1817 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Recife</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Domingos José Martins, João Ribeiro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pernambucan Revolt, Republic of 1817</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1817&amp;focus=republic-of-pernambuco" aria-label="Republic of Pernambuco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3716 2478 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Pernambuco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4036 2734L3845 3279"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3845 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4036 2734h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4036 2734h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Recife. <a href="/atlas?year=1817&amp;focus=republic-of-pernambuco">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
